 "result": "All of it lives in `src/tessel/views.py`, split across two classes.\n\n**`View.to_view_func(name, *class_args, **class_kwargs)`** (L86) turns the class into a function:\n- It builds an inner `view(**kwargs)` closure (L106 or L115).\n- If `init_every_request` is true, the closure creates a fresh instance per request; otherwise it uses one shared instance.\n- The closure calls `active_app.ensure_sync(self.run_view)(**kwargs)`.\n- After applying any `decorators`, it attaches `view_class`, `methods` and `provide_automatic_options` to the function (L129-134). The route registration, `app.attach_route_rule(...)`, reads `view.methods` to decide which HTTP methods the route accepts.\n\n**`VerbView`** (L138) supplies the per-method routing:\n- **`__init_subclass__`** (L165): if the subclass doesn't define `methods`, it builds the set from inherited `methods` plus every name in `http_method_funcs` (get, post and so on) that the class has, uppercased. A class with `get()` and `post()` gets `{\"GET\", \"POST\"}`.\n- **`run_view`** (L182): runs on each request and looks up the handler with `getattr(self, request.method.lower())`. A `HEAD` request falls back to `get` when there's no `head` handler. It then calls the handler via `ensure_sync(meth)(**kwargs)`.",
